# 7. STRUCTURED FIELD MANIFEST

Every analysed document must generate a structured field manifest.

The manifest is the authoritative description of the fields to be created.

Use a documented, versioned JSON schema.

Each field should contain relevant properties such as:

- Unique field ID.
- Internal field name.
- Display label.
- Field type.
- Page index.
- Bounding rectangle.
- Coordinate units or coordinate-space definition.
- Original label text.
- Detection source.
- Confidence score.
- Required status.
- Default value.
- Maximum length.
- Multiline setting.
- Choice options.
- Validation rules.
- Field grouping.
- Relationship to other fields.
- Review status.
- Creation and modification metadata.

Example conceptual structure:

```json
{
  "document_id": "document_001",
  "schema_version": "1.0",
  "fields": [
    {
      "id": "field_001",
      "name": "applicant_full_name",
      "label": "Full Name",
      "type": "text",
      "page": 0,
      "rect": {
        "x": 120,
        "y": 240,
        "width": 300,
        "height": 28
      },
      "confidence": 0.97,
      "required": false,
      "review_status": "pending"
    }
  ]
}
```

The actual implementation must validate this schema.

Use stable field identifiers.

Maintain document and template versions.

Changes must not silently overwrite previously approved templates.

